[{"text":"The paper proposes a solution to the double-spending problem that needs no trusted third party."},{"text":"The paper defines an electronic coin as a chain of digital signatures."},{"text":"The paper says proof-of-work amounts to one vote per IP address."},{"text":"The paper says the proof-of-work difficulty is fixed when the network starts and never changes."},{"text":"The paper says nodes always consider the longest chain to be the correct one."},{"text":"The paper says the first transaction in a block creates new coins owned by the block's creator."},{"text":"The paper says that once a predetermined number of coins have entered circulation, the incentive can come entirely from transaction fees."},{"text":"The paper says a block header with no transactions would be about 80 bytes."},{"text":"The paper says a payment can be verified only by running a full network node."},{"text":"The paper recommends using a new key pair for each transaction."},{"text":"The paper says an attacker with more computing power than the honest nodes could create coins out of thin air."},{"text":"The paper models the race between the honest chain and an attacker's chain as a Binomial Random Walk."},{"text":"The paper says a transaction normally has at most two outputs, one for the payment and one for the change."},{"text":"The paper says nodes must be identified to one another so that messages can be routed."},{"text":"The paper says blocks are generated every ten minutes."},{"text":"The paper cites Adam Back's Hashcash as the model for its proof-of-work."}]